WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:19.480
Along the eastern shoulder of the Cherbourg Peninsula, the beaches of Normandy are strung.

00:19.480 --> 00:28.640
The chilly waters of the channel reach quietly up the sand, which is today as it should be.

00:28.640 --> 00:33.720
A place where people go to spend a holiday afternoon with a cool sea breeze.

00:33.720 --> 00:39.320
There are lasting reminders, however, of a time when these beaches were not so hospitable.

00:39.320 --> 00:43.840
Where the neck of the peninsula joins the Normandy coast lie the beaches American soldiers

00:43.840 --> 00:44.840
remember.

00:44.840 --> 00:51.760
Utah, the westernmost one was called, and the other, Omaha.

00:51.760 --> 00:59.800
To the east, the beach heads the British soldiers can never forget, gold, Juneau, and sword.

00:59.800 --> 01:05.280
Today, history whispers and echoes through empty bunkers and in the imperceptible inroads

01:05.280 --> 01:09.920
of rust flaking from ruined and abandoned implements of war.

01:09.920 --> 01:15.180
To climb the dunes of Utah or Omaha today is quite literally child's play.

01:15.180 --> 01:18.160
It was not so in June of 1944.

01:18.160 --> 01:35.640
After its conquest by the Nazis, France becomes one of the most heavily fortified areas in

01:35.640 --> 01:36.640
history.

01:36.640 --> 01:41.760
But early in the war, plans are made to launch an invasion of this fortress across the English

01:41.760 --> 01:42.760
Channel.

01:42.760 --> 01:48.320
It is given the code name Operation Overlord.

01:48.320 --> 01:52.840
For thousands of American soldiers, the immediate effect of this decision is assignment to Great

01:52.840 --> 01:57.600
Britain to participate in the training and buildup of forces to be used in the invasion.

01:57.600 --> 02:01.400
Not for all will the transfer be so direct.

02:01.400 --> 02:06.880
Some will study invasion on the beaches of North Africa and Sicily and Italy before they

02:06.880 --> 02:09.560
ever set foot on French soil.

02:09.560 --> 02:14.640
Throughout the winter and spring of 1944, great convoys churn across the Atlantic to

02:14.640 --> 02:21.920
British ports.

02:21.920 --> 02:25.840
Into Britain they come by the thousands and tens of thousands.

02:25.840 --> 02:30.000
England in wartime is the land of blood, sweat, and tears.

02:30.000 --> 02:33.880
A nation blackened and battered by the fury of the Nazi Luftwaffe.

02:33.880 --> 02:35.600
But it's still England.

02:35.600 --> 02:41.920
For young Americans on their way to war, there is much to see.

02:41.920 --> 02:45.240
Wartime Britain is a place supercharged with excitement.

02:45.240 --> 02:49.400
Literally the crossroads of the world, filled with uniforms from all the nations united

02:49.400 --> 02:57.120
in the war against the Axis.

02:57.120 --> 03:00.240
But there is work to be done here, and hard work.

03:00.240 --> 03:10.400
The grueling days and weeks and months are devoted to it.

03:10.400 --> 03:19.120
The training has one specialized objective, how to assault a hostile, fortified coast.

03:19.120 --> 03:23.240
The American, British, and Canadian troops who train on England's landing beaches come

03:23.240 --> 03:28.200
to know well what it is they are getting ready for.

03:28.200 --> 03:31.000
The equipment they will use is ready too.

03:31.000 --> 03:38.360
Thousands upon thousands of vehicles, weapons, supplies of every conceivable sort.

03:38.360 --> 03:43.000
On the other side of the channel, the Nazis also know what the Allied forces are preparing

03:43.000 --> 03:45.120
for.

03:45.120 --> 03:47.880
And they are making preparations of their own.

03:47.880 --> 03:52.360
Along the coastline they have built an Atlantic wall, which Adolf Hitler says will make Europe

03:52.360 --> 03:54.400
an impregnable fortress.

03:54.400 --> 04:11.560
The beaches and waters off them are heavily mined and booby-trapped and strung with obstacles.

04:11.560 --> 04:16.880
Behind them, heavy guns are embedded in steel and concrete, positioned to train on any invasion

04:16.880 --> 04:20.560
fleet which might appear in the channel waters.

04:20.560 --> 04:25.840
By the spring of 1944, the Nazis know that an Allied invasion is imminent, but they cannot

04:25.840 --> 04:29.560
tell when it will come or where.

04:29.560 --> 04:34.200
They think the attack most likely will strike where the distance across the channel is narrowest,

04:34.200 --> 04:35.480
at Calais.

04:35.480 --> 04:40.320
Consequently, their strongest forces and fortifications are concentrated here.

04:40.320 --> 04:44.280
The actual target is one of the best kept secrets of the war.

04:44.280 --> 04:49.000
The attack will come along a 50-mile stretch of coastline in the province of Normandy,

04:49.000 --> 04:54.400
sweeping west from the Orne River to a beach on the Coton-Tin Peninsula, dominated by the

04:54.400 --> 04:56.600
vital port of Cherbourg.

04:56.600 --> 05:01.000
The assault is first scheduled for May, when weather conditions are expected to be at their

05:01.000 --> 05:05.520
best.

05:05.520 --> 05:09.800
Allied leaders planning overlord are aware of Nazi estimates that the invasion will strike

05:09.800 --> 05:10.800
at Calais.

05:10.800 --> 05:21.160
They further this deception by ordering heavy bombing in the Calais area, as well as in

05:21.160 --> 05:24.880
Normandy.

05:24.880 --> 05:46.480
In the months preceding the invasion, the tempo of air attacks steadily increases.

05:46.480 --> 05:50.920
Air fleets with their cargoes of destruction thunder out of British bases to pound at oil

05:50.920 --> 06:04.400
centers and industrial plants and airfields.

06:04.400 --> 06:21.920
In these months, the mighty Nazi air force is reduced to a ghost of its former power,

06:21.920 --> 06:30.960
and Allied superiority in the skies above France is won.

06:30.960 --> 06:34.360
Expansion of transportation centers and rail lines is an important part of the invasion

06:34.360 --> 06:39.720
plan in order to isolate the battle area in Normandy and hinder the enemy's efforts to

06:39.720 --> 06:41.720
bring up men and supplies.

06:41.720 --> 06:46.400
It becomes clear that additional time will give the air force a better opportunity to

06:46.400 --> 06:52.800
accomplish this critical objective.

06:52.800 --> 06:57.120
It also is decided to expand the original invasion plan, which called for an attack

06:57.120 --> 07:01.800
along a three-division front into one along a five-division front.

07:01.800 --> 07:05.080
This makes the acquisition of more landing craft essential.

07:05.080 --> 07:09.680
For these reasons, D-Day is postponed from early May until early June.

07:09.680 --> 07:14.400
The men who will launch the attack continue their training through the cold early English

07:14.400 --> 07:20.200
spring.

07:20.200 --> 07:24.680
And then, inevitably, comes the day when all the training and rehearsals are over.

07:24.680 --> 07:28.640
It is time now for the curtain to lift on the real thing.

07:28.640 --> 07:34.920
Throughout May, the roads of England are clogged with convoys as men and supplies begin the

07:34.920 --> 07:39.560
move down to the ports and the loading areas.

07:39.560 --> 07:56.200
The English people cheer them on and pray for them as they pass.

07:56.200 --> 08:09.240
By the end of May, virtually all of southern England is one vast staging area.

08:09.240 --> 08:12.040
The realities of life now are small ones.

08:12.040 --> 08:16.200
For the individual man who will be called upon to execute the plan for the greatest

08:16.200 --> 08:21.840
assault ever proposed in the history of warfare, that plan comes alive now in a myriad of final

08:21.840 --> 08:22.840
details.

08:22.840 --> 08:46.840
Dozens of items to be issued and checked.

08:46.840 --> 09:02.680
Aboard the ships go the hardware of war, cargo which will be indispensable to the fighting

09:02.680 --> 09:07.320
man if he is able to secure a toehold on the hostile beach and expand it into a lodgment

09:07.320 --> 09:08.320
area.

09:08.320 --> 09:13.240
Vehicles and the fuel to run them, rations and fresh water to drink, medicine, weapons

09:13.240 --> 09:18.760
and ammunition, bulldozers and bridges, the long invisible line of equipment which stands

09:18.760 --> 09:22.120
behind every statistic of victory.

09:22.120 --> 09:27.680
Finally, the very heart of the striking force completes the movement.

09:27.680 --> 09:32.280
The men who will spearhead the invasion board the ships which will carry them to their long-awaited

09:32.280 --> 09:34.080
hour of decision.

09:34.080 --> 09:39.560
In file after file they come in all the ports where the invasion fleet lies at anchor, moving

09:39.560 --> 10:05.680
up the gangplanks in a long, solemn shuffle whose cadence becomes the heartbeat of history.

10:05.680 --> 10:11.360
The extensive movement from shore to ship, swiftly and effectively executed, is a masterpiece

10:11.360 --> 10:13.480
of logistical operation.

10:13.480 --> 10:18.440
But with the mechanics of logistics done, the eye of destiny is now on these men who

10:18.440 --> 10:43.480
board their vessels bringing only what they can carry on their backs and in their hearts.

10:43.480 --> 10:48.400
The faithful hours that lie beyond the darkness ahead belong to them to do it as they can.

10:48.400 --> 10:52.440
While the world waits in wonder and hope.

10:52.440 --> 10:57.520
They may not see themselves as the sword bearers of a crusade, but they know well enough what

10:57.520 --> 11:15.760
depends on their effort and the ultimate preparation is for each man to make himself.

11:15.760 --> 11:19.560
Now begins a new period of waiting for the one final word.

11:19.560 --> 11:24.120
An invasion ship is a lonely ship, writes a correspondent who is there.

11:24.120 --> 11:29.560
It is a strange and suspended world, but heavy with memories of the more familiar world left

11:29.560 --> 11:52.320
behind.

11:52.320 --> 11:56.680
Unknown to the men and the tense atmosphere of the invasion fleet, tension is also high

11:56.680 --> 11:59.200
at Supreme Headquarters.

11:59.200 --> 12:04.280
Scheduled for June 5th has been postponed 24 hours because of the worst channel storm

12:04.280 --> 12:06.000
in 20 years.

12:06.000 --> 12:11.000
Now weather conditions predicted for the 6th are far from ideal, but another postponement

12:11.000 --> 12:16.320
will mean a wait of at least two weeks for the right conditions of moon, tide, and weather.

12:16.320 --> 12:19.120
The possible consequences of delay are staggering.

12:19.120 --> 12:23.920
Loss of secrecy, drop in morale, strengthening of enemy defenses.

12:23.920 --> 12:26.240
One man must make the decision.

12:26.240 --> 12:28.560
General Dwight D. Eisenhower, Supreme Commander.

12:28.560 --> 12:30.200
He makes it.

12:30.200 --> 12:56.840
The invasion will go as scheduled on the 6th.

12:56.840 --> 13:20.800
A great armada begins its ponderous move into the channel waters.

13:20.800 --> 13:25.400
The men who will actually be the first into France are still back on English soil.

13:25.400 --> 13:31.440
Paratroopers of the US 82nd and 101st Airborne Divisions and the British 6th Airborne Division

13:31.440 --> 13:35.920
are to jump behind enemy lines to secure the flanks of the invasion area.

13:35.920 --> 13:39.960
On the eve of the invasion, the Supreme Commander visits some of them.

13:39.960 --> 13:44.880
I found them in fine fettle General Eisenhower is to write later, admonishing me that I had

13:44.880 --> 14:01.560
nothing to worry about.

14:01.560 --> 14:06.000
Theirs is to be the biggest airborne invasion ever attempted to this time.

14:06.000 --> 14:11.360
The crucial hours of darkness ahead will make almost superhuman demands on them.

14:11.360 --> 14:15.560
Bad weather and anti-aircraft fire will scatter many of them over a much wider distance than

14:15.560 --> 14:16.560
is planned.

14:16.560 --> 14:21.400
They will be forced to fight the enemy as they find him in the night, in small groups

14:21.400 --> 14:23.400
and on unfamiliar terrain.

14:23.400 --> 14:27.480
But dawn will find the flanks at either end of the Normandy battlefield held by these

14:27.480 --> 14:28.480
first invaders.

14:28.480 --> 14:46.080
None of this, of course, can they know as they board their planes in the late hours

14:46.080 --> 15:02.280
of the night.

15:02.280 --> 15:06.320
The great ghost fleet lifts from its British bases.

15:06.320 --> 15:12.440
The carriers with the airborne warriors.

15:12.440 --> 15:29.040
The gliders carrying weapons and heavy communications equipment.

15:29.040 --> 15:49.480
Voyage of the invading forces.

15:49.480 --> 16:17.600
The midnight skies over Normandy are suddenly alive with the dark shapes of men who will

16:17.600 --> 16:32.160
bring the first fires of D-Day to Fortress Europe.

16:32.160 --> 16:36.600
In the waters of the English Channel, dawn lights up the invasion fleet plowing steadily

16:36.600 --> 16:38.480
toward the Normandy coast.

16:38.480 --> 16:56.640
With almost 5,000 sea-going vessels, the historic armada is the greatest ever assembled.

16:56.640 --> 16:58.680
The sea is incredibly rough.

16:58.680 --> 17:02.760
For many of the warriors aboard the vessels pitching helplessly on the rolling water,

17:02.760 --> 17:28.440
sea sickness is a cruel enemy to be fought before the Germans are ever faced.

17:28.440 --> 17:33.000
An hour before the first troops are scheduled to hit the shore, a naval bombardment opens

17:33.000 --> 17:47.040
up on the enemy's coastal defenses.

17:47.040 --> 18:04.080
Tom hiding between the rocks at the other end, the soldiers start pounding.

18:04.080 --> 18:32.800
The Nazis are at first confused about the Allied invasion.

18:32.800 --> 18:39.800
They are not aware a full-scale assault is in the making.

19:03.800 --> 19:16.800
Allied air forces, which have been protecting the assault convoy throughout its trip, join the attack on enemy targets.

19:16.800 --> 19:21.800
In wave after wave, they roar overhead above the Armada.

19:21.800 --> 19:27.800
In the ships of the invasion fleet, the men can hear the steady pounding of their fire on the enemy's positions,

19:27.800 --> 19:32.800
and they wonder, hopefully, if anything can be left alive on shore.

19:57.800 --> 20:13.800
In a world of nausea and fear and tension, the trip to the beaches begins.

20:13.800 --> 20:18.800
At the spear tip of this historic seaborne attack are some 3,000 men,

20:18.800 --> 20:28.800
American, British, and Canadian troops, who will hit in the first wave.

20:28.800 --> 20:48.800
Soon the main body of ground forces will land in succeeding waves.

21:58.800 --> 22:24.800
The British and Canadians are to take the three eastern beaches.

22:24.800 --> 22:28.800
The Americans are to secure the two on the west.

22:54.800 --> 23:12.800
H-hour.

23:24.800 --> 23:35.800
H-hour.

23:35.800 --> 23:58.800
On beaches called Utah and Omaha and Sword and Gold and Juneau, they come ashore.

23:58.800 --> 24:02.800
On the murderous sands of Normandy and its rim of watery hell,

24:02.800 --> 24:08.800
they push against the gates of fortress Europe, and the fates of war and freedom await their performance.

24:33.800 --> 24:48.800
Enemy resistance varies in degree from beach to beach.

24:48.800 --> 24:53.800
But for every man there, the new world of Normandy is a world of private agony and chaos,

24:53.800 --> 24:57.800
and no man can see the broad design beyond it, on a scale so epic

24:57.800 --> 25:04.800
that words written long ago by William Shakespeare could have been composed for the 6th of June, 1944.

25:04.800 --> 25:11.800
He that outlives this day and comes safe home will stand a tiptoe when this day is named.

25:11.800 --> 25:20.800
Three hours after the invasion begins, it is clear that the Allies have their foothold, however precarious.

25:20.800 --> 25:27.800
The electrifying announcement is made in London.

25:27.800 --> 25:32.800
It is night across the United States. Swing ship workers in war plants are the first to hear the news.

25:32.800 --> 25:38.800
Under the command of General Eisenhower, Allied naval forces supported by strong air forces

25:38.800 --> 25:43.800
began landing Allied armies this morning on the northern coast of France, unquote.

25:43.800 --> 25:46.800
This is the only word we have at this time.

25:46.800 --> 25:57.800
There is no indication yet of exactly where the invasion took place or how many men were involved.

25:57.800 --> 26:06.800
The news comes to America on this June day of 1944, bringing a swell of hope and pride, and anxiety too,

26:06.800 --> 26:20.800
that great victories in war must always be fought dearly.

26:20.800 --> 26:34.800
And the price paid this day for the Allied hold on the beaches of Normandy is not cheap.

26:34.800 --> 26:39.800
Although the cost is steep, the results are incalculable to the plan of battle.

26:39.800 --> 26:43.800
The beachhead soon expands from foothold to lodgment.

26:43.800 --> 26:46.800
Men and weapons and equipment pour ashore.

26:46.800 --> 26:52.800
Within 12 days, almost 600,000 men and 90,000 vehicles are on the beaches,

26:52.800 --> 27:01.800
masked for the drive inland.

27:01.800 --> 27:17.800
Ten months of war still lie ahead in Europe, bitter fighting through the hedgerow country of the lodgment area.

27:17.800 --> 27:22.800
Hard, grinding combat in the cities that lead from Normandy to the banks of the Elbe.

27:22.800 --> 27:24.800
Many men yet will fall.

27:24.800 --> 27:42.800
Many reverses will be suffered, many triumphs recorded.

27:42.800 --> 27:47.800
But in the main, the progress of battle will follow the bold plan conceived in Allied councils

27:47.800 --> 27:51.800
and brought to flaming light by the men who stormed the Normandy beaches

27:51.800 --> 27:54.800
on a day which will forever be known as D-Day,

27:54.800 --> 28:23.800
who unlocked the gates of fortress Europe so that a monstrous evil could be pursued and destroyed.

28:23.800 --> 28:32.800
Ambassador Sir F. S. Brinton

